    drivingExperience: "The E34 518i is the epitome of a beautifully engineered, comfortable, but incredibly slow executive car. The M40 engine produces just 113 horsepower, which is tasked with pulling a heavy, steel-bodied 5-Series. Acceleration is leisurely at best; highway merging requires full throttle and deep forward planning. However, once up to speed, the E34 chassis shines. It is remarkably stable, exceptionally comfortable, and highly refined. The 5-speed manual gearbox is mandatory to wring out any usable performance, as the 4-speed automatic makes it painfully sluggish.",
    mechanicVerdict: "The M40 engine in the E34 is a highly flawed, strictly economy-focused powertrain. The cast-iron block is immortal, but the cylinder head is a nightmare. It harbors two massive vulnerabilities: the timing belt and the camshaft. If you do not change the timing belt strictly every 40,000 km, it WILL snap and destroy the engine. Furthermore, the oil spray bar clogs, wearing the camshaft lobes flat and causing the notoriously loud 'M40 tick'. If you buy a 518i, you must treat the valvetrain with extreme paranoia. Keep the oil pristine, change the belt religiously, and accept that it will never be a fast car. If you want classic BMW performance, look for an M50 inline-6 instead.",
    repairCostEstimate: "$400 - $800 / year (The M40 is mechanically simple, but the strict 40k km timing belt intervals add up. Parts are incredibly cheap, but labor can be high if you don't DIY. Budget for frequent timing belt kits (60 EUR), distributor caps, and constant oil changes to protect the fragile camshaft. If the cam is already ticking loudly, budget 500 EUR for a top-end rebuild or roller-rocker conversion)."
